  "raw_text": "Step 1: Start by setting up the inequality: \\(x^2 - 5x - 4 \\le 10\\).\n\nStep 2: Move all terms to one side to form a standard quadratic inequality: \\(x^2 - 5x - 14 \\le 0\\).\n\nStep 3: Factor the quadratic expression: \\((x - 7)(x + 2) \\le 0\\).\n\nStep 4: Determine the roots of the equation \\((x - 7)(x + 2) = 0\\), which are \\(x = 7\\) and \\(x = -2\\).\n\nStep 5: Use the roots to divide the number line into intervals: \\((-\\infty, -2)\\), \\((-2, 7)\\), and \\((7, \\infty)\\).\n\nStep 6: Test a point from each interval in the inequality \\((x - 7)(x + 2) \\le 0\\):\n- For \\(x \\in (-\\infty, -2)\\), choose \\(x = -3\\): \\((-3 - 7)(-3 + 2) = (-10)(-1) = 10 > 0\\).\n- For \\(x \\in (-2, 7)\\), choose \\(x = 0\\): \\((0 - 7)(0 + 2) = (-7)(2) = -14 < 0\\).\n- For \\(x \\in (7, \\infty)\\), choose \\(x = 8\\): \\((8 - 7)(8 + 2) = (1)(10) = 10 > 0\\).\n\nStep 7: Since we need \\((x - 7)(x + 2) \\le 0\\), the solution is where the expression is less than or equal to zero, which is the interval \\([-2, 7]\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{[-2, 7]}<|im_end|>",
